<BaseLayout title="Transmission" description="T56 Magnum, 4L60/4L80, reverse lights, and pilot bushing notes for LS Gen Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Transmission</h1>
  <p>
    Manual (T56 / Magnum XL) and automatic (4L60E / 4L80E) are both used successfully. Reddit and Facebook builds show everything from budget 4L60 dailies to Magnum XL track cars.
  </p>

  <h2>T56 Magnum XL Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ford-input Magnum XL cases need a custom pilot bushing unless you fit a GM input shaft.</li>
    <li>McLeod 8-1094-2 pilot bushing (Oilite, ~1.094&quot; OD × 0.672&quot; ID, GM except Pontiac style) is a documented choice.</li>
    <li>Clutch alignment tool: 26-spline tools such as the Ram unit sold via American Muscle work for alignment.</li>
  </ul>

  <img src="/images/mcleod-pilot-bushing-mrr-8-1094-2.jpg" alt="McLeod MRR 8-1094-2 pilot bushing in packaging" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    McLeod 8-1094-2 pilot bushing (MRR packaging) — used with Ford-input Magnum XL.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ram-clutch-alignment-tool.jpg" alt="RAM Clutches 26-spline clutch alignment tool" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    RAM 26-spline clutch alignment tool (American Muscle item 34050) for centering the clutch disc during install.
  </p>

  <h2>Automatic Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>4L60E appears on budget daily builds; 4L80E is the usual upgrade when power climbs or for turbo plans.</li>
    <li>Terminator X transmission control needs correct VSS setup and brake-switch wiring for torque-converter lock/unlock (see Wiring and ECU pages).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keeping a Genesis Manual?</h2>
  <p>
    Adapter-plate ideas to keep a stock Genesis 6-speed surface occasionally on Reddit, but nearly all completed swaps use a GM-pattern gearbox (T56 family or automatic) with a shortened or custom driveshaft and proper mounts.
    Stock Genesis trans length and bellhousing pattern make a clean bolt-in unrealistic for most builders.
  </p>

  <h2>Axles</h2>
  <p>
    Genesis Sedan axles are a popular upgrade into the 500–600 hp range.
    One documented failure: CV joint broken at ~700 hp / 650 tq on the dyno — hard launches at the track may need stronger parts sooner.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Part numbers (use -LAM, not -TAU V8):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>LH / driver: 49600-3M000 (or GSP NCV397099)</li>
    <li>RH / passenger: 49650-3M000 (or GSP NCV397100)</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
